   241	"I have walked the ruts," said Seln. "All the way round, at dusk. It is sixteen hundred paces and a little more. I found no person and no sign of one, and I shall walk it again before light. That is a fact about the ruts. If I had laid this, I should not be on the ruts tonight either. I should be a quarter of a mile back from them, on my face, in grass of this colour, and I should come up to them when you were over that stone and not before. So I shall tell you what I can see, which is nothing. And I shall tell you what I should assume, which is that they are here."
   242	
   243	"All of that, and we go," said Brom.
   244	
   245	"We ruled it at a fire. I am saying how."
   246	
   247	He gave it to them a piece at a time, as he had once given five people the order of a watch.
   248	
   249	"We go at first light. We go in file, ten yards apart, down the old road: myself, and then Lira, and Karis, and Cael, with Brom last. The bow is spanned before we cross, with a bolt in the groove, and Brom carries it. Each of us has a day's water and a day's bread on his back. I have known a morning's errand to take a day." His eyes went to the boxes. "Nothing else goes over. The three boxes stay at this stone with both beasts."
   250	
   251	"My paper is in the middle one," said Karis.
   252	
   253	"It will be there at noon. If the floor is as empty as it looks, you will come up for it yourself, and I shall carry it down." He turned to the healer. "You said that somebody should stand outside the line."
   254	
   255	"I'll stand at that stone with my sleeves up. I said it, and I meant it." Oryn was sitting on her heels. "I've learned what my hands are worth on the far side of one. If a thing goes wrong in there, you bring it to me. It's two hundred yards. I've seen an arm carried as far."
   256	
   257	"Then this stone is the objective, as the other was. Whatever is seen, whatever is heard, the word is *Back*, and every one of us comes to it by the straightest way." Seln looked at each of them in turn. "It is a good plan for a place with nobody in it. I do not know of a good plan for the other."
   258	
   259	He looked at Cael last.
   260	
   261	"I said a thing at the fire in the meadow, and I told you that I should say it again here. If there are hands, in the dark, at arm's length, you do as you think fit. If there are eyes, you are a boy with a knife. Nothing that a pair of eyes could carry to a boat."
   262	
   263	"I heard it."
   264	
   265	"I am saying it to five people now, so that when you stand still with all of us looking at you, four of them will know why."
   266	
   267	Karis had got up while he was speaking. She went to the middle box and undid its cord, and lifted the lid a hand's breadth, and felt down the inside of it without a light. What she drew out was a single leaf. Cael knew it by its ruling, even by the fire: six rows, and a numeral struck through at the head of the sixth, and three lines of small exact writing against it.
   268	
   269	She folded it once and put it inside her coat, against her shirt, and tied the box again.
   270	
   271	"There is nothing else in there that says a word about him," she said, to nobody. "That one goes where I go."
   272	
   273	He wrote the night's log with his back against the old iron.
   274	
   275	*Hundred and thirteenth day, night. At the edge of the second one. A stone, and two bars, both numbered thirty-two; one five cold terms old, one a cold term or two, with a mark under its number that Seln won't read. A floor with no stones on it, and a hole, and a stair. Nobody on the ruts.*
   276	
   277	*Tide: once, on the healer, on the ridge road. I asked twice today and she granted the second. The short count and then a minute of the long, at one sitting; I am counting that as one, since it was one asking and one yes. At three there were banks. I knew the Path by ten. In the minute, one channel and one thing in it, going somewhere. It cost her nothing that she would notice. It cost me the use of that hand for an hour, and most of a loaf.*
   278	
   279	*She says not tonight for the other, and she is right.*
   280	
   281	*Deployment since the Line: fourteen.*
   282	
   283	He slept a little toward morning. When Brom woke him the stars were going out, and the frost lay so thick on the old road that it showed like a chalk line all the way down to the floor. Seln was coming back along the ruts from the north, and he shook his head at them from thirty yards away.
   284	
   285	They ate cold. Brom put his boot into the stirrup of a healer's bow, and set the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch, and he laid one of the five bolts in the groove. Karis had her lamp and her notebook and a flask, and Lira drew the knot of her sling tight with her teeth.
   286	
   287	Then Seln stepped over the stone.

