     1	# Chapter 54 — What Stays
     2	
     3	They went in at first light on the hundred and nineteenth day, with a mist lying on the reeds as thick as wool.
     4	
     5	Seln stepped over the grey stone first. Lira went second, with the healer's bow hanging from her right hand by its stock, spanned, and one of the five bolts held in the groove under her thumb. Karis had a roll of thin paper under one arm and her pencils in her hair. Cael walked fourth, and Brom behind him, with nothing on his back for the first time in three days and his right arm bound across his chest. The healer came last, with a pannier on her shoulder and her sleeves pushed up.
     6	
     7	Five sigils had gone out at the stone, one after another, and nobody had remarked on it. Nobody spoke on the path. The reeds stood over them on both hands, white with rime, and forty feet out on the left the stems leaned apart and closed again, keeping their pace.
     8	
     9	At the path's end the healer stopped.
    10	
    11	She stood on the kerb, where it was two strides broad, and set her pannier down between her feet. She was a long way inside the line, with no Path to count by, and she knew it. She folded her arms, and looked across thirty yards of water at a gap in a grey wall.
    12	
    13	"I'll be here," said Oryn. "Walk."
    14	
    15	Seln stepped down.
    16	
    17	The water came to the middle of his shin. He did not stand in it; he went straight on, at the pace of a man crossing a yard, and the four of them went down after him one by one. The plates were slick under Cael's boots, and each of them gave a little as his weight came onto it, and rocked back as he left it, so that it was like walking over the lids of a row of great chests. He counted his own strides. He did not let any one of them be longer than its fellow.
    18	
    19	The knocking came round the dish from the right when they were halfway.
    20	
    21	He heard it draw level, and fall in behind, and follow. It was no nearer than it had been in the reeds. A swell went with them across the last ten yards, a little to the left of Brom's heels, and Brom did not turn his head to look at it; Cael could see the back of his neck, which was red, and the care with which he was putting down each foot.
    22	
    23	The gap in the wall had a sill of the grey, a hand above the water. Seln went over it without breaking step, and the rest of them after him.
    24	
    25	It was a room. He could see that much while he walked, and he took it in without stopping. It was twelve strides each way. The walls were a yard through, and they stood three times Brom's height at the far corner and were broken down to the height of a man's chest in two places on the left, with every break worn as round as a river stone. There was no roof and there never could have been a beam; the mist lay across the top like a lid. And the inner face of every wall was cut. The sunk lines ran in their courses from the water's skin to the broken tops, course over course, straight and hooked and crossing, nine deep where the wall was whole.
    26	
