+## Chapter 54 — What Stays
+
+Six scenes.
+
+1. First: `They went in at first light`  
+   Last: `and found nothing to close on.`  
+   Entry order and Seln’s transfer of bow and hand are clear. `bow` is BOH for the crossbow throughout this scene. Clean.
+2. First: `Brom had been told that he`  
+   Last: `one of them could see it.`  
+   The attack is traceable by sound and position. In `head as blunt as the bow of a skiff`, `bow` is BAU (the front of a boat), contrasting with every weapon `bow` in the chapter; direction must pin this. Speaker roll call is explicit. Clean.
+3. First: `"It let us walk in," said`  
+   Last: `tell. He did not get up.`  
+   Seln’s rule, Karis/Lira’s copying exchange and Brom/Cael warning sequence remain attributable. Clean.
+4. First: `Oryn was in the dish before`  
+   Last: `how, on both heels, and stood.`  
+   Oryn’s rescue logic and Cael’s silent selection of Anchor are clear. The italic rule is an inward recalled finding. Clean.
+5. First: `He gave the read the floor.`  
+   Last: `long as a boy could hold.`  
+   Seln counts overhead; Lira’s cut-off warning is identified by name. Cael’s `Brom. Wall. Your weight. Now.` is then deliberately relayed by Seln, and the narration says so. Clean.
+6. First: `Lira did not look at the`  
+   Last: `one pace, as they had come.`  
+   Bow choreography is legible: Lira looses twice, Seln spans and shoots twice, then one bolt remains. `mill-race` is the water channel. Oryn/Brom exchange and final sheet count are clear. Clean.
+
+Restored/protected Hall-result unit, second-ear ruling:
+
+- Before: `"What did we get?"` Unit: `"Walls," Karis said, from the coping. "I've the walls. Twelve sheets."` After: `Nobody proposed going back for anything.` Brom’s question names the purpose of the return; Karis’s answer and the immediate refusal to re-enter make the result land cleanly. Sound.
+
+## Chapter 55 — Twice
+
+Five scenes.
+
+1. First: `The healer had Brom down on`  
+   Last: `and let me see that face."`  
+   Oryn’s medical orders and Brom’s answers are clear. The protected `Twice` unit is deliberately embedded in narration, then Brom and Oryn each repeat the word; the following narration explicitly names each repetition. Clean.
+2. First: `Cael had not moved from the`  
+   Last: `I said that I'd say when."`  
+   Oryn/Cael exchange is cleanly alternating and the `So are you` route admission lands without speaker doubt. Ten-day ribs clock and three-reading plan are clear.
+3. First: `They made two fires of it,`  
+   Last: `Line: twenty-six.* *We left it alive.*`  
+   Karis, Seln and Lira actions are separable. The joined Lira bow speech remains one thought. Italic cost log is introduced; three listed Hall deployments correctly advance the total from twenty-three to twenty-six.
+4. First: `He read himself that night when`  
+   Last: `asleep before the pencil was dry.`  
+   Cael’s self-reading clearly distinguishes the inner still place from the external Quieting. `mill-race` is the water channel. Protected log text remains document register. Clean.
+5. First: `He woke while the stars were`  
+   Last: `leave.* *Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*`  
+   Oryn directs the reading and counts it; `One`, `Twenty`, `Forty`, `Sixty` are hers and are explicitly tagged. Cael’s answers remain alternating and clear. `wound` in `It isn't a wound` is WOOND. The final Tide deployment correctly advances twenty-six to twenty-seven.
+
+Coordinator-restored consent sentence, second-ear ruling:
+
+- Before: `Nobody went to her.` Restored sentence: `Cael had heard her say on a mountain that her reserve was a purse; he had watched her empty it at a mill, and at two stones, and he understood by now that what she spent on such a morning was the very thing that a child at some northern gate was waiting for, and that she knew it to the grain.` After: `"Twice. It'll hold twice. It will not hold three times this season, so don't ask it to" — and Brom said, "Arm," and she said, "No," and he didn't.` The restored sentence preserves the ethical cost and gives the protected limit its necessary stakes; it is long but hierarchical and sound.
+
+Restored/protected `Twice` unit, second-ear ruling:
+
+- Before: `Cael had heard her say on a mountain that her reserve was a purse; he had watched her empty it at a mill, and at two stones, and he understood by now that what she spent on such a morning was the very thing that a child at some northern gate was waiting for, and that she knew it to the grain.` Unit: `"Twice. It'll hold twice. It will not hold three times this season, so don't ask it to" — and Brom said, "Arm," and she said, "No," and he didn't.` After: `"Twice."` The narrated attribution inside the protected sentence makes Oryn and Brom explicit; the next paragraph is identified as Brom immediately afterward. Sound; do not alter the unusual punctuation.
+
+Lane-B speech join, second-ear ruling:
