    59	She unrolled it on the lid of the box and set a pebble on each corner. There were three rings on it still, and one of them was a lie: she had drawn it on the ninety-second evening round a place where a carter lifted his hand, and since noon she had known to a stride where it ought to have been. She took the crumb of bread that she kept for such work and rubbed the old ring out. She drew the new one a finger nearer to the corner, where a cord and a girl's left foot had put it, and wrote the day beside it, and *moved; paced.*
    60	
    61	Then the middles. She had stood on all three. One was the centre of a white floor with eleven stones about it, and one was a square hole with a stair in it, and one was a spring. She made a dot in each ring with the point of her pencil, as small as she could make it.
    62	
    63	She took the brass out of its cloth, and laid it down.
    64	
    65	It touched the three dots.
    66	
    67	She did not trust that, and she set about breaking it. A straight thing will lie through any two marks on a skin, and so two of those dots proved nothing whatever; the whole question was the third. She moved the brass. She laid it through the Fallow's dot and the hall's, and looked at the Stair's: it sat under the edge. She laid it through the Stair's and the hall's, and the Fallow's sat under the edge. She took her pencil and made each dot again a pencil's breadth to one side, which was as far out as she believed that she could be on a map of days, and laid the brass a fourth time, and a fifth.
    68	
    69	It would not miss. She could make it lie a hair to this side of a dot, or a hair to that; she could not make daylight show between the brass and any of the three.
    70	
    71	And she had not chosen the third. She had laid that edge on two rings at a grey stone, in daylight, with five people looking, before any one of them had seen a reed. It had run off into a corner of the skin where a man once lifted his hand. They had walked along it for three days, in a strip a mile wide, and had come over a rise onto a marsh.
    72	
    73	There was no arranging a test of that kind. She had laid the ruler herself, before the third place was known, and then passed through the answer on her feet.
    74	
    75	She wrote the line on the first leaf, under the walls, and then on the second, and the third.
    76	
    77	*The three middles lie on one straight edge, as near as this skin can show.*
    78	
    79	It was entered. She sat and looked at what lay above it. There was a perimeter, three times, to the stride. There were forty-one kinds, and none strange. There was a line on a wall that was divided to a circle's radius, and stakes that were numbered in a row. And now there was a ruler lying through the three of them.
    80	
    81	She had laid leaves side by side on a trestle once, before it was light, and had known what they said when they lay so. There had been three of them. It had been a smaller thing that morning, and she had been able to put a clean leaf back into a box.
    82	
    83	There was one line of the page left, under the last. She knew what went on it. It was not a long sentence, and every word of it stood ready, and her hand was not quite steady; she observed that, as she would have observed it in anybody.
    84	
    85	Across twenty yards of dark, at the other fire, a man had sat down on the ground beside a boy, and got up again.
    86	
    87	The pencil came down to the clean line, and stopped.
    88	
    89	---
    90	
    91	She laid it out for them on the hundred and twenty-first morning, in daylight, on the lid of the middle box, with all of them looking.
    92	
    93	There were more than six. Teague had come down off the rise with the first of the light to say that he was going, and he stood a little outside the ring of them, with his thumbs in his belt, as a man stands at a neighbour's fence. Karis did not seem to mind him. She had the hide flat under four pebbles, and the three rings on it, and Cael saw that the third was not where it had been.
    94	
    95	"I have moved one," she said. "It is entered that I moved it, and why. These are the middles."
    96	
    97	She showed them the three dots. Then she laid the brass down, and took her hands away from it.
    98	
    99	"Three points make a line. Four make an argument."
   100	
   101	It was said in the small exact voice, and it was the only sentence that she gave them. She did not tell them that she had tried to make it miss. Cael knew that she had, since he had watched a strip of brass go up and down a calfskin by firelight for the better part of an hour; and he knew by her face that it had not obliged her.
   102	
   103	"The man with the book wrote *on bearing*," said Seln. He had not taken the ledger out. "He drew through two, as she said in that room, and he had a third confirmed along it. Hers lie along the same three. I think that we may call it one line, and his."
   104	
   105	"It's got numbers on," said Lira.
   106	
   107	She had been frowning at the brass. She put out her right forefinger, and touched the skin beside each ring in turn, going down the edge.
   108	
   109	"Thirty-one. Thirty-two. Thirty-three. They're in a row. I'm not saying what it counts; she'll not let me. I'm saying which way it counts."
   110	
   111	"That is an inference about an order," said Karis. "It says nothing of what is being ordered. I shall enter the order."
   112	
   113	"So there's a thirty-four," said Brom, from his blanket. He had been propped against the right-hand box, strapped like a parcel. "On along it."
   114	
   115	"There is a fourth, or there is not. I cannot argue from three." Her eyes had gone down the brass, past the last dot, to where the bright edge ran out over bare hide and off the skin. "It is not on this map. He never had a wheel so far."
   116	
   117	They were all looking at Cael by then, since it was his to rule.
   118	
   119	He went over it as he went over anything. There was meal for six days in the left-hand box, and a little bacon. There was a shoulder that was bound and would not be mended a third time, and an arm in a sling that was to hold nothing till the snow. The frost had come back in the night, harder than they had yet had it, and a healer had said at a stone that the snow would not be long. And there was a line. He had been walking toward something for a hundred and twenty days without knowing that it had a direction, and it had one now, and it went off the edge of a calfskin.
   120	
   121	"Three days out along it," he said. "No farther. If there's nothing under the brass by the third dusk, we turn for Lowmarch, and I'll say so then without anybody having to ask me."
   122	
   123	"And if there is?" said Lira.
   124	
   125	"Then we look at it from outside, and we go home, and we have an argument."
   126	
   127	Nobody spoke against it. Teague had heard the whole of that, and he had asked nothing; but Cael saw his eyes go once along a strip of brass, from one end to the other, and stay for a breath on the place where it left the hide.
