+# Book 7 — line and listening proof, part 4 notes
+
+Reader: Codex, independent fresh-context listening seat, 2026-10-11. Scope: chapters 46–60 only. Each chapter is read in full, in order, from numbered output. Scene boundaries below quote the first six and last six whitespace-delimited words exactly as printed in the manuscript (markdown retained where it falls within the six words).
+
+## Chapter 46 — Doors Open Both Ways
+
+Seven scenes.
+
+1. First: `Seln asked for the other paper`  
+   Last: `walked past a paper that paid.`  
+   Contract-council voices remain distinct: Seln owns the documentary analysis; Brom, Lira and Karis are tagged at entry. The document phrase *The ground opens* will flatten as quoted writing but its introduction makes the register clear. No numeral or homograph stumble.
+2. First: `"Each of you says it," said`  
+   Last: `have learned to say those aloud."`  
+   Lira/Brom/Karis are explicitly called in order. Brom’s long horse account cannot be mistaken for the bowman or another man because Cael calls `Brom` immediately before it and Lira answers him by content. Clean.
+3. First: `"Seln," said Cael. "Take it." "They'll`  
+   Last: `to learn afterward that we went."`  
+   Seln’s quiet desire and Cael’s argument are clearly divided by `It was Cael's turn`; no listener attribution issue. The protected `Six years I've wanted a face` stands and is not a finding.
+4. First: `Brom had got up while he`  
+   Last: `it when it was against you."`  
+   Vote sequence is explicit. The joined Lira exchange at the end alternates for only one line each and is clear. Four hands plus Lira’s non-vote remains audible.
+5. First: `He carried the paper back up`  
+   Last: `Teague stood aside from the door.`  
+   Pike’s `HOLD.` is visibly all-caps and should be read as the word “hold,” not spelled. Pike and Teague are separated by the narrated approach and tags. `lead it off` earlier in the chapter is LEED; no ambiguity in context.
+6. First: `On that same night, the hundred`  
+   Last: `was true or only well put.`  
+   Seln POV. Italic dictated wording is introduced before it appears; document register clear. Clean.
+7. First: `It was not revenge, though he`  
+   Last: `been lying, which was still warm.`  
+   Seln’s private audit is coherent; pronouns retain antecedents after lane cuts. Clean.
+
+Lane-B speech joins, second-ear ruling:
+
+- ch46:59. Before: `I don't need it read to me a third time.` After: `There's a walk on that oak at fifty that nobody's scratched, a string of salt to the middle holds before the pass shuts.` Same Lira speech, continuous argument; sound.
+- ch46:119. Before: `I did not say that it was wise to answer it.` After: `I was asked what I would do, and I have said.` Same Seln speech and same qualification; sound.
+
+Restored/protected council unit, second-ear ruling:
+
+- Before: `He looked round the four of them, one face at a time.` Protected unit: `Inside the circle, I'm the only one who works. They chose a floor that makes the rest of you helpless. They don't know it doesn't do anything to me.` After: `It was the first time that he had said it to all of them in so many words.` The lead-in announces Cael’s turn and the follow-on confirms the disclosure; sound.
+
+## Chapter 47 — Millrace
+
+Five scenes.
+
+1. First: `They were out of Lowmarch two`  
+   Last: `come to the door. "I know."`  
+   Oryn’s offstage `I know` is anchored by `The answer came from a lean-to`; after the route references, the listener can identify her. Millrace and Lowmarch remain distinct. Clean.
+2. First: `There was a bench along the`  
+   Last: `Oryn crooked a finger at him.`  
