+# Book 7 — line and listening proof, part 4 notes
+
+Reader: Codex, independent fresh-context listening seat, 2026-10-11. Scope: chapters 46–60 only. Each chapter is read in full, in order, from numbered output. Scene boundaries below quote the first six and last six whitespace-delimited words exactly as printed in the manuscript (markdown retained where it falls within the six words).
+
+## Chapter 46 — Doors Open Both Ways
+
+Seven scenes.
+
+1. First: `Seln asked for the other paper`  
+   Last: `walked past a paper that paid.`  
+   Contract-council voices remain distinct: Seln owns the documentary analysis; Brom, Lira and Karis are tagged at entry. The document phrase *The ground opens* will flatten as quoted writing but its introduction makes the register clear. No numeral or homograph stumble.
+2. First: `"Each of you says it," said`  
+   Last: `have learned to say those aloud."`  
+   Lira/Brom/Karis are explicitly called in order. Brom’s long horse account cannot be mistaken for the bowman or another man because Cael calls `Brom` immediately before it and Lira answers him by content. Clean.
+3. First: `"Seln," said Cael. "Take it." "They'll`  
+   Last: `to learn afterward that we went."`  
+   Seln’s quiet desire and Cael’s argument are clearly divided by `It was Cael's turn`; no listener attribution issue. The protected `Six years I've wanted a face` stands and is not a finding.
+4. First: `Brom had got up while he`  
+   Last: `it when it was against you."`  
+   Vote sequence is explicit. The joined Lira exchange at the end alternates for only one line each and is clear. Four hands plus Lira’s non-vote remains audible.
+5. First: `He carried the paper back up`  
+   Last: `Teague stood aside from the door.`  
+   Pike’s `HOLD.` is visibly all-caps and should be read as the word “hold,” not spelled. Pike and Teague are separated by the narrated approach and tags. `lead it off` earlier in the chapter is LEED; no ambiguity in context.
+6. First: `On that same night, the hundred`  
+   Last: `was true or only well put.`  
+   Seln POV. Italic dictated wording is introduced before it appears; document register clear. Clean.
+7. First: `It was not revenge, though he`  
+   Last: `been lying, which was still warm.`  
+   Seln’s private audit is coherent; pronouns retain antecedents after lane cuts. Clean.
+
+Lane-B speech joins, second-ear ruling:
+
+- ch46:59. Before: `I don't need it read to me a third time.` After: `There's a walk on that oak at fifty that nobody's scratched, a string of salt to the middle holds before the pass shuts.` Same Lira speech, continuous argument; sound.
+- ch46:119. Before: `I did not say that it was wise to answer it.` After: `I was asked what I would do, and I have said.` Same Seln speech and same qualification; sound.
+
+Restored/protected council unit, second-ear ruling:
+
+- Before: `He looked round the four of them, one face at a time.` Protected unit: `Inside the circle, I'm the only one who works. They chose a floor that makes the rest of you helpless. They don't know it doesn't do anything to me.` After: `It was the first time that he had said it to all of them in so many words.` The lead-in announces Cael’s turn and the follow-on confirms the disclosure; sound.
+
+## Chapter 47 — Millrace
+
+Five scenes.
+
+1. First: `They were out of Lowmarch two`  
+   Last: `come to the door. "I know."`  
+   Oryn’s offstage `I know` is anchored by `The answer came from a lean-to`; after the route references, the listener can identify her. Millrace and Lowmarch remain distinct. Clean.
+2. First: `There was a bench along the`  
+   Last: `Oryn crooked a finger at him.`  
+   Oryn’s patient-directed speech is consistently separated by physical treatment and tags. The old woman’s `The third` answer follows Oryn’s correction and is clear by alternation. Counts are words and medically purposeful. Clean.
+3. First: `"You," said Oryn. She was speaking`  
+   Last: `that goes nowhere but a ford?"`  
+   The boy’s wrist request is unambiguous: Cael asks Oryn, Oryn refuses, Cael steps back. Karis’s quiet aside is tagged and the transition back to Oryn’s patient check is cued by `a hoarse voice from the lean-to`. Clean.
+4. First: `Cael told her, since it was`  
+   Last: `much less that is than enough.`  
+   Oryn’s route arithmetic stays intelligible aloud; every figure is spelled out. Cael/Oryn alternation is brief and semantically secure. Clean.
+5. First: `"Now you'll hear what it is,"`  
+   Last: `with the white sun over them.`  
+   Terms, bowl exchange and mule business retain clear speakers. The italic day log will flatten as a document; its introduction is explicit. Clean.
+
+Restored/protected units, second-ear ruling:
+
+- Refusal. Before: `She put it by as he had seen her put it by before.` Unit: `"No."` / `"You asked. That's the rule and you kept it. The answer's no. Next."` After: `He stood for as long as it took him to be sure that he had nothing to add, and he had nothing.` The separate paragraphs give the refusal its proper silence; Oryn is the only possible speaker; sound.
+- Karis’s observation. Before: `Her voice was pitched for his ear alone.` Unit: `"And then at her own wrist. I have seen that look on a person in front of a shut press. She was curious about you. Enter that somewhere."` After: `"It's not mine to enter."` Karis is named immediately before and Cael’s answer makes the ownership distinction audible; sound.
+
+## Chapter 48 — Two Stakes
+
+Five scenes.
+
+1. First: `"Well?" said Oryn. She had called`  
+   Last: `ten, and took his hand away.`  
+   `May I read you?` and Oryn’s granted terms are unmistakable. Italic inward counts flatten cleanly as Cael’s internal count. `mill-race` is the water channel, distinct from Millrace by syntax and stress.
+2. First: `"Well, I'd hope so," said Oryn.`  
+   Last: `him the rest of the loaf.`  
+   Oryn directs the long read and counts `Forty` and `Sixty`; the count is spelled and the speaker tagged. Lira and Brom’s closing one-line exchange is explicitly tagged. Clean.
+3. First: `They came to the end of`  
+   Last: `I'll pace it in the morning."`  
+   Stair geography and the Quieting boundary are one-pass clear. No confusing `as` or number echo.
+4. First: `It was Lira who found the`  
+   Last: `if that quiet one lets you."`  
+   The two stake ages and repeated number thirty-two remain consistent. Seln’s `Newer` line is his finding, not a new unnamed speaker. `wind` in `wind had dropped` is WIND; no WYND use here.
+5. First: `The quiet one did not let`  
+   Last: `Then Seln stepped over the stone.`  
+   Seln’s order is long but remains his throughout; Brom, Karis, Oryn and Cael are tagged when they answer. Karis’s protected leaf is described but not read. The italic night log and deployment line are introduced and should take document register. Clean.
+
+Lane-B speech join, second-ear ruling:
+
+- ch48:241. Before: `That is a fact about the ruts.` After: `If I had laid this, I should not be on the ruts tonight either.` Same Seln report, with the second sentence drawing the inference from the first; the removed folded-hands beat is unnecessary and the join is sound.
+
+Restored/protected consent unit, second-ear ruling:
+
+- Before: `It was hers to open. He saw that, and he saw also that she would not have him take a thing that he had not asked for; if he was to have it, he must ask it outright of the woman who was sitting on that rock.` Unit: `"May I read you?"` / `"Yes. That's the answer, and I'm the one giving it, and I want you to notice that it's the same day as the lean-to and a different question, and that the rule held both times. Put your hand on it. Surface first. Count of ten. Don't go in past the count until I say."` After: `Lira had stopped chewing, and Karis, who had her notebook half out of her coat, pushed it back in.` The lead-in establishes that Oryn must initiate reopening but Cael must explicitly ask; the aftermath gives the permission its weight. Sound and morally unambiguous.
+
+## Chapter 49 — Forty Steps
+
