editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:25:She read that over, and left it. It was true, and it was less than she wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:35:She had her own reach for each of the three, in cords. What she wanted beside it was the other man's: the figure that a surveyor had gone over twice with his pencil, in a room under a floor, until it cut the paper. She had heard it read out once. She had written it on her cuff, in the dark of that room, and the cuff had since been in a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:45:She understood that well enough. She had lived for years beside shelves that she was allowed to read at and forbidden to carry from. And he had been the keeper of one shut thing for twelve years; it was not to be supposed that he would take up a second and hold it open to everybody. He had come twenty yards across the turf with it, unasked, which was a great deal farther than she had expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:79:It was entered. She sat and looked at what lay above it. There was a perimeter, three times, to the stride. There were forty-one kinds, and none strange. There was a line on a wall that was divided to a circle's radius, and stakes that were numbered in a row. And now there was a ruler lying through the three of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:109:"Thirty-one. Thirty-two. Thirty-three. They're in a row. I'm not saying what it counts; she'll not let me. I'm saying which way it counts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:He did it on purpose. He had counted that crew on a hundred roads without thinking; today he looked at each of them as he would have looked at a room that he was to leave in the morning. Seln went far out in front, small against the sky, with a case on his chest and a book in his coat. Karis walked with the hide rolled under her arm, and her lips moving. Lira had the healer's bow slung across her back on its strap, and she set the great boot down whole at every stride; she had been given no order to hand that bow back, and she had not offered. Brom led the brown mule on her low side, with the kit on his left shoulder and four yards of webbing holding the rest of him together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:193:"I owe the rest of you something. She's had it, as she set it, so it's free." He kept it short. "There's a place in me, under the breastbone, where nothing moves. All ten fragments go round it. I've read it three times, and it keeps. I don't know what it is, and I've been told to stop touching it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:9:The road climbed from there. By the time there was grey enough to see by they were on the back of a long ridge, bare and broad, with pale grass on it that the wind had combed all one way. The country fell off on both hands. To the east it was hills beyond hills, hatched with the red-barked trees that Karis would not name. To the west the ground went down in long folds toward a valley that he could not yet see into, and a line of mist lay along the bottom of it like wool caught on a fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:49:"That's one," said Oryn. "You'll not close it on a rope before the thaw. Send in the child."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:105:"I've read one thing with what I had from you, and it was myself. I found no bank in it anywhere, as you found none. I don't know what a bank is like under the hand. I've only your word that there is such a thing." He kept his own hands at his sides. "When you take the boy's wrist, may I set my hand on it too? I want to learn what reaches you through yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:241:They forded below the mill, and the woman on the flat stone counted six of them and eight hooves across without lifting her net; and they climbed out of the green valley into the wind again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:247:He read it over. Then he looked at six people on a bare ridge, and at the left-hand ruts, which ran on to the south with the white sun over them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:79:He went over it as he went over a load. Six people had lain round that low ground for a day with their faces covered, and had gone off it north and south and west when a boy stood up with a light. They had gone as people go who have practised leaving. He had read where they lay, by lantern, and had found nothing above the height of a boot. People who take such pains over what they leave behind are not people who go home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:89:"For them," said the bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:105:She had put her spoon down. He knew her opinion, since she had given it to him over the bow's hip more times than he could count. A thing put right in an hour by a Path was a thing that nobody afterward thought to tie. Hands were quick, she said, and quick was not the same as held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:117:It kept a road for the first of them, and a grown-over track for the second, and he read the six as he went. The big one was carrying on his left side. The girl was setting her right foot down whole, at every stride, and had been told to. The small one stopped at each halting-place and knelt, and laid something on her knee; and the quiet one, whom he had needed two looks to find in an inn, had been out in front of the rest the whole way, alone. On the third morning the track gave out, and the trail did not so much as waver. That was what he could not get past. People go to water, or to a roof, or along whatever road there is. These had gone over wet grass with no mark to steer by, round nothing, toward nothing that he could see, as a plumb line hangs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:123:"Well?" the bow had said, when he came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:135:There was a camp at the track's end with two fires. He counted his own first: a spear, and a bow, and a boy, and an old woman with a board. Then he counted the camp, and there were six in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:165:The tenth did not stir, since it never asked for anything. But he knew what was within his reach, as a man with a key in his pocket knows a lock when he passes one. It would take two fingers, and a count of ten. At three he would have this man's banks under his hand, and at ten he would know the Path as he knew a friend's step. In a minute more he would have what ran in it, and how deep. That was the Bronze which a one-armed man had guessed at under his eave, and had never been told. A registrar would have given a year's pay for what lay a foot from his fingers. He could have had the whole of it and told nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:169:Nor did he ask whether he might, and he saw why as he sat there. There was only one person whose leave could be asked, and that was the man himself; and to ask him was to ask that he hand over, to a boy at a fire, what he had kept out of every book that was ever opened on him. A healer had told Cael at a well that asking was the least of it. A hand went onto a person when there was something to read *for*. He had nothing to read this man for but the wish to know what he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:203:*Teague sat down next to me. Asked what I am. I could have read him. I asked instead. The day I stop asking is the day I'm the registry. Write it down. Again. Every time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:5:Cael took it out of Hesk's book, where it had lain since the ninetieth night with five strokes at its foot. Seln held the two sheets up to the fire, one in each hand, so that the flame stood behind both; and for some time he looked through them, as he had looked through the first on an inn's boards, and read no word of either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:25:"There is a second thing," said Seln. "Read me the place again. Only the place."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:35:Nobody at that fire needed the list read out. There was a Wind on it, and an Iron Skin, and an Ember, and a Shadow, each with a tier and a rank, in files that had been kept for years on the far side of the Line. And there was a boat gone west ten days ago to say that every one of those four had walked onto a certain kind of ground and been able to do nothing there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:47:He said it to the fire, in the mild voice in which he had once read out a floor schedule, and then he sat back out of the light. Cael understood him well enough: a broker's clerk had stood at a plank for a hundred days and watched which crew took which paper, and it had wanted only one fact about a boy to write the second sheet; the boy had supplied that fact himself, aloud, at an inn's boards, when he showed that he had never yet walked past a paper that paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:59:"He's just told you what it is. It's got a ruled edge and a word for itself. I don't need it read to me a third time. There's a walk on that oak at fifty that nobody's scratched, a string of salt to the middle holds before the pass shuts. There's a fence at forty, the day the hold comes off it. That's ninety marks of work that was posted by people who want a fence. We'd be down from both before the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:81:"My father knew horses. I'll give him that; it was the one thing that he did know. He went all over that animal with his hands for an hour, and there was nothing wrong with a leg or an eye or his wind. So he stood there. I can see him standing. He knew that a sound horse doesn't go for a third of his price, and he knew that he'd found nothing, and the two wouldn't lie down together." Brom's big shoulders moved. "And then he reached, since it was three hundred for one, and he could not bear to see another man lead it off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:85:"It went over backward. Not every day. It would carry you a fortnight like a lamb, and then it would stand straight up on a hard road and come down on its own saddle. It broke his collarbone, and a groom's leg, and the groom never walked right after." He picked the stick up again. "The dealer hadn't cheated him. That's what I've kept of it. The dealer had priced that horse to a penny. It was my father who read the figure as luck."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:131:"Seln's read the paper right, and Brom's read the number right. It's a trap, and it's been cut to our measure by somebody with our files open. Everything in it has been reckoned." He kept his voice low. "But it stands on one belief, which is that a certain kind of floor does the same to all of us. And that's the one thing about this crew that nobody in the west can find out. They sent a paper to learn whether we'd come out of the first, and we did, and what they've learned from it is wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:173:"I'll read it over your arm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:213:Then he wrote one thing more. He set it above the strokes, in the square capitals that he kept for it, and Cael read it upside down as it was made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:267:Seln could hear the dictation in it. *South of Lowmarch; the first day.* A clerk left to himself would have put *one day south*. Somebody had stood at that man's shoulder with a surveyor's notes in his hand and read them out a stop at a time, and the clerk had set them down as they came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:289:Now there was a fifth grade, which nobody had taught him, and he had held it in his two hands tonight. It was a lure, and a lure was a different kind of thing from the other four. The record could be kept by nobody in particular, and the instrument read by any clerk; the knock came through a door and the hand out of a ditch. But a lure that is taken must be met. Somebody had to be there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:23:Lira held nothing; she sat on the grey stone with the bow across her knee, and looked from the healer's hands to the reeds, and back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:57:And his eyes would not hold a near thing. That was the read, which he had leaned on harder than ever in his life, through water and stone, and when he looked at his own hand it swam.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:79:"Lie still. I want to read you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:85:"I know what I'll get. I've had it six times." She was counting already, under the words. "I've had my hands on every one of them this morning, to see what that place had done. I'll not sit down to my bread with one left out, only because he can't be read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:95:"They're not gone again; you'd not have walked that path. They've been leaned on, by a thing that I'd not like to put a weight to." She sat back on her heels. "I can't shut what I can't read. You've heard me say it till you're sick of it. That's ten days of knowing that you've ribs, and they'll talk when it turns cold, and they'll not do more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:103:"I told you a night that's only a night, and I meant one with nothing in front of it. You've not had one since, and you'll not get one on this road. But I've been looking at you for a quarter of an hour, and there's another kind." She nodded at his chest. "You read it frightened, in a loft. Tonight there's nothing left in you to be frightened with; I doubt that you could be afraid of a wolf. So you'll read it tonight, when the rest are down. Then you'll sleep, and you'll read it once more when you wake, before you've had time to think. That's three, on three days, with a night between each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:121:Lira had not left the grey stone. She sat on it with her foot set down whole and the healer's bow along her knee, with its nose toward the reeds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:125:"Seln says that a night won't hurt it." She did not turn round. "There's one. If I let it down, I've a stick with a string on, and nobody here to bend it who isn't asleep or mending. It stays in the bow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:127:"It's not coming out of that water. He read the mud."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:129:"I know what he read. I watched it come up out of a hole this morning, the whole of it, when a carter had said that nobody ever saw so much. I'm not saying that it's coming. I'm saying that I've one, and it's in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:131:He left her to it, since she had sat on a stair for a day once with that same bow, for the same reason, and had been right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:155:*Hundred and nineteenth day. The track's end, outside the stone. All six out. Brom's shoulder opened on a wall and is mended a second time, an hour; it will not be mended a third. Four bolts spent; one in the bow, with Lira.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:157:*The read: the floor, through water and a plate. All of it, for three counts of warning.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:169:He read himself that night when the others were down, by her order, and he was an hour about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:175:At ten the short reading gave him his own gait, quick and slow, and tonight a good deal more slow than quick. He went on beyond ten, into the deep reading, and it was easier than it had been in a loft, since he had nothing in him tonight that could hurry it; he went along the dry hiss of the Wind at a walk, looking for a side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:177:He knew now what he was looking for. He had been in a river with banks, on a ridge, for a minute, and had felt them stand close and high on either hand like the walls of a mill-race. He went the whole length of the Wind and found none. The millstone was under it, and ran into it, and he could not have said where; he tried the wick, and the bellows, and the held note, which was thinner tonight than he had ever heard it, and they were ten, and not one of them had an edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:203:*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:209:He woke while the stars were still out, with frost on his blanket, and read it a third time before he had fairly remembered where he was. He was rested, and cold, and clear in the head, which was a third temper and as unlike the loft as it was unlike the night before; and the place lay where it had lain, with the ten going round it by the same worn way. It took him less than a quarter of an hour, and he wrote one line afterward by feel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:227:"You're going to tell me about a thing that I'll never get my hands on. I'd take a finding from a second healer across a table, if I had felt the patient. I've not felt this one. I've had a river in the dark off you seven times, and that's all that I'll ever have." She spoke slowly, sorting it. "But you were in me for a minute, on that ridge, and I felt you go along. I know your step. If you're in me while you say it, I'll have the thing that found it, under my own skin, while it tells me what it found. It's the nearest that I'll come."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:235:"I'm asking, then. Will you let me read you, now, while I tell it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:237:"Yes. Hand on the wrist. The short reading first, to ten, and then the neck, and the long reading for one minute, which I'll count. You'll talk while you're in. If I say *out*, you come out with the sentence in your mouth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:239:He put his right hand on her wrist, which was broad, and cold, with the white scar across the heel of the hand; and the tenth turned toward it, and went. At three there were the banks, close and high, and at ten he knew her, as a person knows a step on a stair. He laid his left palm on the back of her neck, under the cropped hair, and she let out one long breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:243:He was in, with the single bed about him, and the one deep thing in it, running. It was the strangest place in which he had ever tried to tell the truth: he went along a healer's river at his own uneven pace, with her banks standing close on either hand, and said aloud to her what the same going-along had found three times in a body that had none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:291:"It isn't a wound."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:11:The six on the rim were where they had been all day: two on the brow above him, two away to the south, one north, and one far off across the low ground against the last of the light. The two on the brow were on their feet; the one with the bow had it up across his chest, spanned, and the other was holding his own hands out before him and looking into them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:17:The bow came round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:19:He had priced this on the stair. There was one bolt in that groove, and when it was gone, a boot must go into a stirrup and a hook onto a string, and a back must straighten; he had watched that man do it once, from a frozen road, and it had taken him something over twenty counts. He had been told on a bluff never to spend the Wind near a thing whose single blow he could not price. He could price this one to a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:23:*Front. Back. Shoulders.* He loaded nothing on the third. He stood through it, with Wind lying quiet in his soles, and he watched a hooded shape forty paces up a hill. A girl had come down off a gate-walk eight days ago with a sore palm and three words, which a lean man had given her. Shooting was stopping. The stock on the brow settled into a shoulder. The shape behind it grew smaller, as a person does who is letting out his breath. And then it was entirely still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:31:*One.* Everything that he owned went dumb. For the length of that count there was a boy standing on a hillside who could not have shifted a foot to save his life; and he spent it looking up at the brow, since his eyes were the only part of him that would answer. The man with the bow had not stood to watch his bolt. He had the nose of the thing on the turf already, and his boot going into the stirrup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:49:He had run up a ditch on the Fallow in that lag, with hounds behind him, and he knew it; but he had not known what it was to close with a man in it. His boots struck the turf, and then he was told that they had struck. The brow came up to meet him. There was a hooded shape in a cloud of chaff with one arm across its face, and it had let the string go half drawn and caught the bow up by its stirrup end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:59:The hooded man had both his hands stung off the bow. It fell between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:85:It was the bowman who ended it. He had got the chaff out of his cloth, and he bent, without haste, and took up the bow from the turf. He did something to the lock of it with his thumb, and a small dark thing like a walnut came away into his palm and went into his coat. He drew a short knife across the string, and then he laid the bow down again in the ruts, carefully, on its side, as a workman lays down a tool that was only lent him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:163:"I can't touch that. You know why." She threw him what was left of Lira's tape. "Wind it round, and keep it out of the frost. It'll close faster than it ought to. Yours always do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:169:"I tied those to be read. There is a stem of grass laid under every knot, and each is where I laid it." He set the lamp down. "Six people with a quarter of a mile of rim apiece do not leave it to look into a mule's load."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:179:He brought it into the lamplight and turned it over, slowly, as he turned over any paper. It was a good bow, heavier than the healer's, with a prod of horn and sinew, and its string hung cut from one end. He ran his thumb along the underside of the stock, and under the stirrup, and across the butt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:181:"There was a maker's stamp here. It has been taken off with a file, and the place oiled. There is no armoury number. It was bought for one day, by somebody who meant from the first to leave it lying." His voice had not risen. Cael had never heard it so quiet. "I walked past the place where that man lay, twice. I have sat in a hole since sunrise with his bolt on the stair. I have his bow. And I could not tell you tonight whether he was tall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:191:Cael opened Hesk's book on his knee. Lira had put it into his lap when she came over the stone, with the strap done up, and had said nothing; and he held the pencil in his bandaged left hand, since the right would not close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:201:*The read: the ground, from the hole to the bar. All my eyes. I looked up once, and it left me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:207:*Wind: one burst, off the stone. The lock.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:227:It was a lantern, and it was carried high, at the length of somebody's arm, as a person carries one who wishes it understood from a long way off that he is not creeping. Seln was on his feet before Cael had seen it. Lira had the bow across her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:247:Teague's eyes did not leave him, and Cael waited for the rest of it: how, and with what, and what a boy was who had a Wind's crack in him and a fire as well. He watched the man weigh each of those and set it down again, as he had set down a question at an inn's door two nights before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:277:Pike said nothing more. He wrote the name. But he kept his one hand on the shingle for a breath after the ink was dry, and Teague knew that he had been read; and he found, somewhat to his surprise, that he did not mind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:281:But he could be half a day from where a certain paper pointed, with a spear and a bow and a boy, on honest business. Nobody could say that it rested on nothing, since it rested on three stakes and four marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:283:They walked at noon, and slept at the fork above the mill, without a fire, a crew's length apart; and before he lay down he counted his three by their breathing, as he did every night of his life. The bow's was the short one, since that hip had not liked the ridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:297:"Look for what?" said the bow. He was easing his leg out along the turf, a little at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:305:The spear said nothing, which was what he said to most things. The bow grunted, and went on easing his leg. The boy put the bread into his mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:311:He set two stakes with the spear and the boy, in water to the thigh, while the bow sat on the bank and sharpened the third. He did the sum as he drove them: it was an afternoon up to that place, by the ruts, and a morning to go in and bring a case out, if it was a walk and a carry, so that they would be down at the ford again by the middle of the day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:325:Not one of the three asked him why. The spear took his spear. The bow got up off the bank, on the good leg first, and slung his stave; and Teague, who had been about to tell him that he might stay with the mill and the mules, saw his face and held his tongue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:331:There was a light first. It was small, and low down, where the dark ground was; a point of fire that did not flicker as a brand flickers. Before he had fairly taken it in, there was a sound that he knew as well as his own name, since he had walked eleven cold terms beside a Wind, long ago, in another crew. That crack was a Wind's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:339:He stood on the ridge with the wind in his face and counted what he had. A Wind was one thing. An Ember, who could make fire stand on the air, was another, and he had worked beside two such; and whatever had gone up that ditch was a third. He had seen all three within the space of forty breaths, at one spot, and when he looked for the people who had done them he could find only one upright shape, very small, with its arm raised.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:347:He read the ground by that lantern as they came down, since it was the thing that he could do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:351:That was a crossbow. He had expected a bow of some kind. He had not expected that every sign of its owner would stop at the height of a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:363:The bow had the three of them across his knee before they were fairly on the grass. He turned each to the lantern, and tried each empty lock with his thumb, and shook his head over them like a man at a horse fair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:381:He put the spear on the southern rim, where two had stood, and sent the boy west, since the boy had the legs for it. The bow he set on the north, which was the shortest walk, and he took the brow himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:5:It was the hundred and fifteenth morning. He had slept as a stone sleeps, and woken to find that the ground came to his feet when it should, and that his right arm would bend. His left palm was wound in what remained of a roll of tape. He had counted four upright shapes on the rim before he had his boots on, one at each of the places where a hood had stood the day before, and he had been on his feet with nothing in his hands before he saw whose they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:93:The walls were cut from the floor to the roof. The work ran in courses, one above another, five to a wall, each as tall as a forearm; and in every course the fine sunk lines stood side by side in their groups, straight and hooked and crossing, as close as the words on a page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:121:"One person came to this. I have his track in from the door, a little to the left of ours, and the dust has lain on it a long while. He knelt here, and here, and I cannot find where he went out." He glanced up. "That may be the dust; I say only that I can read his coming and I cannot read his going. There has been nobody since, and nothing is tied to it, and nothing is under it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:183:"Read it," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:187:"There is a line at the head, set by itself, where a place is named." Seln held the leaf to the lantern for some time. "There are four signs, and after them the number that is on that bar. The number I can read; the signs are from an index, and it is not one that I ever used, or was ever shown."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:209:He read it out, and she wrote it down without turning her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:211:"Below that he has a few lines on the floor and the stair, such as she has in her own table. I shall read them to her when she has a hand free." Seln turned the leaf. "And on the second page there is one line only."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:221:Seln read it as it stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:251:Karis worked along the walls on her knees, and then standing, and then on Brom's bent back, which he offered without being asked and would not hear refused. She did not rub everything; she went down each course with her lamp held close and her lips moving, and wherever a group of strokes stood that she had not yet got on paper, she put out her hand behind her. Twice she left the paper altogether, and walked the foot of a wall with the short cord in her fist, counting the divisions within one line against the cord's scale rather than the distance along the stone, and wrote a figure where nobody could see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:269:Cael wrote, with his left hand, as he had written the stations. Seln read him the slips of bark out of the middle box, one after another, every group that had been drawn at a trestle in a barn; and Karis called from the wall where each one stood here, and whether its bars were close or wide, and he put a stroke against the slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:271:"It is close on both," said Karis, an hour later, in a voice that he had not heard from her before. "It was wide on the ninth stone. So wide was only the ninth stone. It comes out of the bowl, and it goes into the cup with its fellow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:273:The cup and the bowl stood on the lid of the box, where the lamp could reach them; she had carried them from Thornwater in a twist of cloth. One by one, through the afternoon, he watched the grains leave the bowl: some went into the cup, and some she laid on the stone by themselves, in a row, and those were the ones which the second floor had shown her to be no kind at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:295:"This one. It's not on the Ring. I looked for it for four days and it isn't on any upright. It's here, on every wall, and it's not in the courses with the rest — it's *between* them. On its own. Here." The pencil moved along the sheet to the next one, and the next. "And here. And here. I've paced the spacing because I pace things. It comes every — " she looked at the notebook, where she had written it, and read it out as a number she had checked twice — "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, to the stride. It recurs, on the wall, at the perimeter's radius."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:343:He had been over the floor, and through the grass below the stone, and he had an armful of ash shafts, which he laid on the turf in a row beside four crossbows, one of which was in two pieces. Lira drew the one from her belt and laid it at the end of the row. There was an iron head lying by the stone that had no shaft to it; the boy looked at that, and at Brom, and went rather red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:345:Brom had taken up one of the shafts, and he tried it along the stock of the healer's bow, where it stood proud of its channel by the thickness of a finger, and would not lie down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:347:"Too stout. They'll not go in hers." He put it back in the row. "I'd as soon not have them, come to that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:They did not go back as they had come. Seln had read the hide by Karis's lamp on the crest, and had found them a shorter way than a marsh and a stair: along the ridge to the heap of chock-stones, and then north by the hide carters' road, which ran through shut holds toward a river that all of them knew. He walked it ahead of them, as he walked everything. Four times in a day he came back along the file and said his word, and after the second day Karis left off writing down the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:15:On the fourth the wind went into the north and stayed there, and they walked with their chins in their collars and the mule's breath freezing on her whiskers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:25:The foot was taped, from a roll that had been put into her hand at a fork, without a word, by a person who had then walked north; and she wound it herself each morning, sitting on a box, with her one hand and her teeth. Then she set the great boot down whole at every stride of every mile, on ruts that were iron and on turf that was glass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:29:He was three strides behind. He saw the boot go, and her right arm fly out; and he saw the left, under the grey wool, start to come and stop at a knot. She went down on her right shoulder and her hip, hard, with the bow clattering on her back. She lay for one breath with her cheek on the ice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:53:On the hundred and twenty-fourth night, the first of the road home, Seln read a page and a half that he had by heart already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:55:He read it on his watch, as he had on every night since a room under a floor. He sat with his back to four sleepers and the limp little book open on his knee, tilted to what the embers gave. He did not need the light. He used it regardless, since there is a difference between remembering a hand and looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:71:He had written *nothing to report* at the foot of a weekly return for twelve years, over the top of a case, and had thought it the hardest sentence in the service to write honestly. This man had written a better one. He had written what was there. He had put down eight figures that he did not understand, because they were so, and had underlined the one that frightened him; and Seln, who had read some thousands of returns, could not remember another that had been made by a person so little concerned with what his reader wished to hear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:77:It was three days later, on the fourth of that road, with the wind gone into the north, that he saw what he had been doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:81:He had not meant to keep it so. He had put it into his coat in a cold room because it was registry paper. Reading registry paper was the one craft that he had brought east with him. That had been true. It was also true that he had read it to nobody since. A researcher had wanted one figure out of it, at a fire by a marsh, and had not felt able to ask. He had gone to her. He had held the page to the light for her three times with his fingers shut on its spine.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:89:He had spent fifteen years learning to keep things from being read. He could think of no worse shelf for a dead man's true figures than the coat of such a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:131:It came on as they went down, thinly, out of no wind at all. Cael walked through the meadow to the river's edge, to the planks of the landing, and stopped there and counted what he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:133:It was the fifth time that he had read that town, and the first from below. He had always had it from a saddle or a ridge, laid out like a board; now it stood over him. The river went by at his feet, dark, between two shelves of ice that had grown since he saw them last until each was as broad as a cart is long. The ferry's cable sagged across from bank to bank with a white rime on it. The scow lay on the near side, and a man with a mallet was knocking ice from its running blocks. In the doorway of the round shed a brown gelding with one white foot stood under a blanket and watched him do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:171:"So I'd heard." Pike dipped his pen, and wrote, and blew on it. In the last column, where a price went, he set down three things with a stop between each, and read them out as he made them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:197:He took it by its corner and swung it on the bench, so that the writing faced the other way. Cael had read every line of that book upside down for a hundred and twenty days, across an oak plank. It lay the right way up before him now. He read the season in a square black hand, and at the end of it a ruled space with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:5:Seln stepped over the grey stone first. Lira went second, with the healer's bow hanging from her right hand by its stock, spanned, and one of the five bolts held in the groove under her thumb. Karis had a roll of thin paper under one arm and her pencils in her hair. Cael walked fourth, and Brom behind him, with nothing on his back for the first time in three days and his right arm bound across his chest. The healer came last, with a pannier on her shoulder and her sleeves pushed up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:17:The water came to the middle of his shin. He did not stand in it; he went straight on, at the pace of a man crossing a yard, and the four of them went down after him one by one. The plates were slick under Cael's boots, and each of them gave a little as his weight came onto it, and rocked back as he left it, so that it was like walking over the lids of a row of great chests. He counted his own strides. He did not let any one of them be longer than its fellow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:31:"Left," said Seln. "The low place. I go up, and I take the bow from her, and then her hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:33:He went at the first of the breaks without slackening, and put both palms on the round of it, and was sitting on the wall's top before Cael had seen how. Lira held the bow up to him by its stock. He laid it behind him, and took her right hand in both of his, and she walked up the cut face of the wall on her whole sole as though it had been a stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:35:Karis came to the foot of the break with the roll under her arm, and reached up. She was a head shorter than Lira. Her fingers went over the round of the stone, and found nothing to close on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:61:He fell along the tipping plate toward the gap they had come in by, on his left arm, and rolled. Where he had stood there was a darkness as broad as a door, and the two halves of it came together on a yard of water and the skirt of his coat. Cael saw the cloth tear. He saw a head as blunt as the bow of a skiff, the colour of mud, and no eye in it anywhere. Then it had gone down, and the plate dropped back, and Brom was up on his knees in the wash of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:75:Nobody moved, and for the space of ten breaths the only sounds in that place were the spring, and five people getting their wind, and the small knocking going round below them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:79:"Karis," said Lira. "Here; I've the bow. Cael's along from us, at the corner. You're across the hole. Brom?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:85:Cael looked at where each of them was, since there was nothing else that he could do for them. Karis and Lira sat on the northern wall near its low end, with the bow between them. Seln had gone along the same wall-top at a crouch, past Cael, and round the corner, and stood now on the eastern wall, which was the tallest, where he could see the whole of the hole. Cael was at the corner between them. And Brom lay by himself on the far side of the water, on a strip of wall no longer than a cart, which ended at the gap on one hand and at nothing on the other. Its top was green, and wet, since the spring's water slopped over it on the way out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:119:"No, I can see that you're not," said Lira; and she laid the bow down on the stone, and took a fistful of Karis's belt in her right hand, and held on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:127:Brom had got himself up to sitting. He sat astride the low wall as a man sits a horse, with his bound arm against his chest, and he was studying the gap beside him. Cael could read what was in his face. The gap was two strides wide. On its far side the wall began again, low, and ran on to the corner, and round, to where the others were; and at the foot of the gap, between the two ends of wall, lay the sill. It was the old work. A man who stood on it stood on nothing that the thing below could read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:177:Lira had the bow up, and nothing to loose it at but water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:179:He had ten fragments. He went through nine of them in the time of one breath, as he had on a frozen road, and it was different, because here there was nobody to see, and he need hold none of them back. Wind was in his feet already. It would put him over the wall in a count. Then it would lock him where he landed, dumb, for a count more, the stillest thing on that floor, at a time and a place that he had not chosen. Storm would lay the water flat, and what was that to a thing with no eyes? Ember's fire was for things that could see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:191:He gave the read the floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:231:It came after the slab as it had come after a stone, and the slab stayed. He felt the jaws close on the place. Everything that he had went thin. He was on his hands and knees on a plate with water to his elbows, and he could not have said which way the wall was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:233:A hand had him by the collar, and another under his arm. He was dragged up a face of cut stone like a wet sack, with his ribs shrieking, and laid along the top of it. Seln's voice, close to his ear, said that he was to keep hold of whatever he was holding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:253:She had slid down from her knees to the seat of her breeches on the wall-top, with her left leg folded under her and her right knee drawn up. The sole of the great boot was planted whole on the stone. Behind her right shoulder a length of the wall's old coping still stood, a foot higher than the rest. She set her shoulder against it, and laid the stock across her knee, and drew its butt back into the hollow of that shoulder. The knee and the coping held the bow between them, and her right hand had only a lever to mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:257:Her left arm lay where a healer had tied it, high against the collar-bone, and did not stir. A lean man had told her on a gate-walk that a bow wants one hand, and a thing that does not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:269:Seln had the bow out of her hand before the word was finished. He stood up on a yard of stone, with his boot in the iron and the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped. He laid a bolt in the groove from the sleeve at his belt, and put the bow into her hand again, and she had not once moved her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:279:Seln had the bow again, spanned, with the third bolt in it. He had been holding it out to her. He did not answer; he went along the wall-top to the corner at a run, bent double, and round it onto the eastern wall, and Cael saw him bring a crossbow to his shoulder for the first time in two years of knowing him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:303:"It is alive," said Seln. He was coming back along the wall with the bow. "And it is going down, and it is hurt, and I do not know how long any of those will last. Karis. Paper. We walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:315:On the kerb, which was the hall's outer coping, Seln stopped, since a person could. He set his boot in the stirrup once more, and spanned, and laid the last bolt in the groove; and he held the bow out to Lira by its stock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:17:Brom came last. Cael heard him grunt at the stride, as he had grunted on the Fallow, like a man who has felt a door shut behind him. The healer's bow was on his left shoulder, spanned, with a bolt lying in its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:41:Something was wrong with that shot, and though he had only fifteen yards to cover, it took him the whole of them to say what. A bow on that brow could have let all five of them walk down to the floor, and had them two hundred yards inside, with nothing over them and nowhere to go. It had chosen to shoot the last man instead, when he was five strides from the way out, with a healer standing in plain sight at the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:91:There were two on the southern rim, a quarter of the way round. They had risen out of the white grass behind the ruts while he was looking at the brow. One of them was kneeling over a bow, and the other only stood, a little apart, with his arms folded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:109:He had not called them. They had risen as they had risen in a lane on the Fallow, each offering what it had. Wind was in his feet, and though it could not take him through three courses of that fence, it could take him a long way along the inside of it, faster than a hooded man could run round the ruts. Storm, the heaviest of them, lay ready; there was a rut at his feet, running straight up the hill to the brow, with seed on the grass at either lip. Ember had fire for his hand, and there was a great deal that a boy could do from where he stood, in a place where four of his friends could do nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:She did not wait to be answered. She stepped in close under Brom's good arm, and with her right hand she lifted the healer's bow off his left shoulder by its stock, spanned as it was, with the bolt held in the groove under her thumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:177:"Keep on," she said, between her teeth. "I've got it. I've got the bow. Keep *on*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:185:"Stand where you are," said Seln's voice, close to his ear. "All of you. I am striking a light."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:209:"It is what the paper says we were sent for. Six people brought us to this room this morning by a road that they had prepared, and that is lying in the middle of it. It has lain there a good while, by the dust. It will lie there until I have read the floor round it, with more oil than we have." He took the lamp out of Karis's hand and gave it to the healer. "That is for him. Use it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:221:"Why not?" said Lira. She was sitting on the lowest step with the bow across her knees and her left side turned away from the light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:225:Lira looked at the bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:247:Then she became somebody else, as Cael had seen her do once before, at the rim of a white floor, with a strip of her own shirt in her teeth. She took the lamp from Karis and held it close, and looked at the wound from in front and from behind, for a long time, without touching it. She laid two fingers on the inside of Brom's left wrist and counted, with her lips, to a good deal more than ten, and put her ear to his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:267:"It's high. It's missed the lung and it's missed the big vessel, or he'd not be talking. Leave it in, because the bolt's the plug. When my hands work again I'll take it out and close what it opened. Not before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:271:Oryn had it in her hands before it stopped rolling. She laid the first strip round the shaft in front, close, and the second behind; she did it by lamp and by eye, as a carter's wife would have done it, and it took her a quarter of the time that it would have taken anybody else in that room. When she had done she pulled what was left of his coat across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:273:"You'll sit there. You'll not lean on it, and you'll not be brave." She wiped her fingers on her breeches. "I said in a loft that somebody ought to stand outside one of these with her sleeves up. I was outside. I'd my sleeves up. And I'm in here with a roll of tape, and that's a clean wound that I could shut in an hour on the far side of a rock." Her voice was low and quite even. "I came south for exactly this. Enter that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:289:"It was the twentieth step," she said. She was looking at the wall above the healer's head. "The ankle went. I'd the bow in my right hand, so I'd nothing to put out. And then it was out. I never told it to. It hit the wall, and it took me, the whole of me, and I stood up off it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:293:The healer undid her own knot and drew the wool down, and two of the eight small marks had opened, the lowest of each row, and were bleeding steadily.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:7:Lira sat on the thirty-fourth step, six above the floor, with the bow along her knee, and Seln stood below her in the doorway at the stair's foot. Brom was on the ground against the wall to the north of that doorway, with three coats on him, and the healer sat on her heels at his left hand. Karis had put her back against the wall opposite; and in the middle of the floor, where nobody went, a case lay in the dark with its cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:17:"The one at the gate with the bow is doing what you would do. He is finding his range."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:51:Cael had gone to the foot of the stair, where he could see her against the white, a dark shape with one arm, and the bow lying along her knee with its nose toward the square. It had not stirred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:55:"At what? There's nobody in it." She did not turn her head. "I've one in the groove. If I loose it at a patch of sky I've an empty bow that I can't span, and Seln has to come up six steps into the light to do it for me. He'd like that, up there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:59:"I've stopped. I'll go on being stopped till there's something standing in that square." Her right hand moved on the stock, a finger's breadth, and lay still. "It stays in the bow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:83:She had two fingers on his wrist again. She could not read him; she could count his heart as any woman in a steading could, and she did it every quarter of an hour by some clock that she carried in her head, and said the same one word after it, which was *there*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:87:The cold was the worst of it. There was no frost in that room, and no wind; but the stone drank the warmth out of whatever touched it, as dry ground drinks water. Cael had given his coat to Brom, and by the middle of the white he could not feel his feet. He walked the south wall with his fingers on it, ten strides and back, and at the fourth turn his hand met wool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:95:"I am touching it. That is a different thing, and I had better be honest about which." Her voice was small and exact in the dark. "It is cut as the bands on those stones were cut. I can find a hooked stroke with two bars across its stem under my second finger, and I cannot tell by touch whether the bars stand close or wide; so I shall not say that it is the same hand." She let her hand come down. "I have a wall here that I would give a year of my life to read. It is against my spine. My paper is in a box at a stone, and there is a finger of oil in that lamp, and the oil is for his shoulder if it turns. I have gone over that three times, to see whether I could make it come out otherwise. It will not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:107:"I have had since this morning to read it, and you should have my reading. The bolt she read for herself, on the road, and she read it rightly. A crew with a hurt man and a healer at the stone might have fought its way back to her. A crew with a hurt man and a healer who has no hands will sit still." His mild voice did not alter. "There are six of them, for a rim of sixteen hundred paces. That is a quarter of a mile to each station, and nobody to spare, which is why they have not come down; if one leaves his place, there is a gap in the dish that five people might run for. They will not come in, and they do not need to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:125:Cael got up from the wall, and his legs were stiff. He went across to the foot of the stair, to be nearer the light, and found that Lira had turned on her step to meet him, six above the floor, and was sitting with her chin on the stock of the bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:147:"I want to read it out," he said. "The whole design. Stop me where I've got it wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:149:"There's a paper, priced so that we'd come. There's a floor at the end of it where a Wind can't run, and an Iron Skin is a big man, and an Ember can't light her own lamp, and a Shadow has a knife and fifteen years. There's a bolt, placed to bring a healer over a line. There's a fence behind her. There are four bows on a rim, far enough back that we can't reach them and near enough to shoot anybody who climbs. And there's a night." He drew a breath. "Every piece of it does one thing. It takes away what one of us can do. It's a list with six names on it and a line through each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:161:"Then that's what I can't do. I can't shout. If one of them sees a thing work on this side of that stone, there's a boat." He went through them aloud, since four of the people in that room knew them as well as he did. "The Wind cracks. The heavy one would lay the grass flat for thirty feet. Fire's fire; the Pressure can be seen gathering; the edge rings. That's five that shout. The read and the Compression are no use past the length of my arm, and the tenth does nothing at all in a fight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:165:"There's the one that I keep shut. It was made for not being seen. And there's the one that I had from the hand on the Ostrand road." He looked toward the place where the healer was. "I was inside a thing like that fence once, with nineteen points round me. I let it go from where it touched me, and the whole of it came down like a row of stitches. I've done that one time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:187:"You've read it over my arm. If it wants reading again, it's yours first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:227:He had made up his mind about the next thing in the dark. There were ten bolts lying on that grey somewhere, where they had skated and stopped, and one kick would send any of them ringing; so he gave the read the ground, which was the oldest and quietest thing that he owned, and it went out under his soles for its pace and a half, as it always did. It told him what was there, and did not tell him what anything meant. But a spent bolt has a weight, and he felt the first of them lying a yard ahead and a little to the left, like a pencil on a table, before his boot could find it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:245:The read went out from under his feet. He had taken his mind off it for the length of one glance, and his boot came down on a loose stone in the rut and turned it over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:247:It made no sound, since nothing that he did made any sound. But he stood for ten breaths with his heart going, and his eyes in the ground again, and gave the read back to the turf before he took another step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:285:He lay down in the rut on his left side, with the bar at the length of his arm. He put his right hand out toward it, and round it, and could not tell when he had touched it; he had to watch his own fingers close on the scale to learn that they had. Past the iron, his hand was on the far side of that edge, and he laid the dead palm against the outer face, low down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:15:They lay thick in that corner, wavy and close, as he had drawn them nowhere else on the skin. In the midst of them was the round of bare hide that Karis had ringed on the ninety-second evening, with a word beside it. The edge went by that ring, on the side toward the corner, and there was the width of a finger between the brass and her pencilled round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:29:Seln had been reading the hide over her shoulder, as he read any paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:275:She gave it to him before she had thought, and then she stared, and so did the rest of them. He had read out a great many things to that notebook in a hundred days: the width of a road, and the age of a bar, and what a paper meant. He had asked each time that it should be entered as said. He had never once put a mark in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:277:He wrote four short lines, in the small square hand of a man who had filled other people's files for fifteen years, and read them over as he read anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:305:Anchor did not leap, as Wind would have. It went down his arm as a plumb line goes down a well, and there was a point under his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:319:It was like having a tooth drawn. Every other fragment that he owned went thin and far away, Wind and Storm and the tenth together, and the cold of the water, and the kerb under his knees; there was only the one point, and something very strong that wished to take it up, and himself saying that it should stay. The jaws came down a second time, and a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:339:"In a minute."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:341:"Then have your minute. Nobody else is to stop when we go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:349:They made their camp at the track's end, a few yards outside the grey stone, with the two mules hobbled on the rise. It had come on cold again with the dusk. Seln lit the fire, and the six of them sat close about it while Karis said what she wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:351:"The walls are cut on the inner side. I could see the courses through the gap, and I could not have read one stroke of them at thirty yards if I had sat on that kerb for a week. So it is the inside or it is nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:353:"There's a way to do it," said Seln. "It has given us its rule, and a rule is a plan. On the floor, we move. We rest only on what comes up out of the old work: the path, and the kerb, and the tops of those walls, which are a yard through. A wall-top is a kerb set on end." He counted it off. "We go in at first light. Lira carries the bow, spanned, since she can loose it and cannot bend it. I carry the hook and the sleeve. Karis has her paper and nothing else. Thirty yards of plates, at a walk, with nobody standing; in at the gap; and up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:371:Lira had the healer's bow across her knee, and she was looking past the fire at the dark place where the reeds began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:21:She laid them in three heaps along the deal, and did not try to make their edges agree; the Fallow's were long strips, and the Stair's were leaves, and the hall's were whatever a person could tear from a roll with her teeth. There were eleven in the first heap. There were twenty-nine in the second, and twelve in the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:37:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:39:*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Not an entry any more; a location. The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:53:He sorted it aloud. He had done so with every bag that Cael had seen come over, each piece given out twice, so that people who could not read should know what was theirs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:69:He read it standing in the snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:141:She led the jenny in under the gate-walk. A lean man stood up there with a long bow at the length of his arm, and he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had never known him do that for her. She understood, crossing the yard, that it was not for her: it was for a girl with one arm, who had sat on those planks beside him and shot at a sack, and he was asking after her in the only way that he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:193:She could not read an arm from here. She had said as much in a yard, of a nearer gate than this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:225:It was a hand that Cael had never seen. It was square, and stiff, and it pressed, like a person lifting something heavy. He read the four lines out, as they stood, a stop at a time: the route, and a knee, and what three weeks had cost; and then the thaw, and a map, and what he was to do when he was told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:227:Brom had six bowls in a row on the board. He looked along them. He had put the last one down a little apart, as he always did; and he did not take it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:265:Nothing out here had read him. At three floors older than any registry, every Path in the party had stopped at a line and his had not; a fourth had been paced from outside and not crossed. Nobody alive — least of all him — knew why. Someone had made the pattern. That was as far as anyone could say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:5:She had called the halt herself, an hour south of the fork, where a grey rock as big as a cart stood up out of the ridge and broke the wind. The two mules were nose to tail in its lee. Brom had the bread out. She had sat down on a ledge of the rock with her coat pulled round her, and had looked at Cael across the trodden grass for as long as it takes to count ten before she spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:11:"May I read you?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:19:"At that bench you asked to touch a boy while I had his wrist, so that you could feel what I felt. His leave was never yours to ask from me, and his treatment was not your lesson." She pushed back her left cuff. "Here you've asked to read me. This is my wrist, and I can answer for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:23:"You told me yourself, and well enough. You've read one thing in your life, and it's got no banks, and you don't know what one is. You can't tell what's missing from a thing until you've had your hand on one that's whole." Her eyes were level. "It's not to learn whether your fingers work. I'd not sit here for that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:31:It was strange beyond telling to stand so. She had stopped a stride and a half from every person that he had ever seen her meet, until her hands were wanted; and he had been read by her six times and had never once touched her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:45:He nearly took his hand away. In himself, at ten, he had had only his own gait, quick and slow, and nothing whatever to say what it went along in. This went along *in* something. He felt the sides of it first, close on either hand, and high, and as plain as the banks of a mill-race where the stone has been laid by somebody who knew his work; he could have walked along the top of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:73:"You'll have a minute of the long one," she said. "No more. I'll count it, and I'll say when, and you'll come out on my word and not on your own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:77:"I said that I don't run it on a road, since it costs me a mending and I've none to throw about. It's you that's running this, and I'm only sitting under it." She was turning her collar down. "And a minute's no reading. It's a look through a door. But the short one shows you that there's a bed, and it never yet showed anybody what was running in it. I'd not have you go back to yourself knowing the half."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:89:It was the banks again at first, close and high, and he went along between them at his own uneven pace. Then he was past the place where a count of ten would have stopped him, and for the first time he was *in* a person who was not himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:97:That was the difference, and it took him some of his minute to find the word for it. His own went along, quick or dragging, as he was glad or tired, and it had never seemed to him to be going anywhere in particular. Hers was going somewhere: it ran as a river runs in its last mile, when it can smell the sea. Wherever his attention rested on it, he found it already leaning that way, toward his left hand, and then toward his right; it came to meet whatever touched it. It did not pool in any place, and there was no slack water in her at all. The whole of that woman, from the neck to the heels, was on its way *to* something, and had been for so long that the banks had worn to fit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:101:He eased his neck. He had seen a man's shin close under those hands in an hour, and had watched her walk to a mule and unload it, for five days that she could not spare; and he had taken her for a person who gave things up. She gave nothing up. She was made as a road is made, to get to where the hurt was, and everything in her that was not road had been spent on it years ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:133:The carters' ruts did what he had seen them do once before. Halfway down the slope the ground eased into a kind of brow, and at the brow the live ruts swung away to the north and went round the whole of that low place, keeping their distance from it, and came back to their line on the far side. He could see them all the way, two pale threads on the grass, and inside them an older road went straight on down, grown over, with a hundred years of turf in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:149:Nobody moved, and the wind had dropped; in all that low ground there was not a bird, or a beast, or any smoke; there was a bare grey floor with a square cut in it, and three steps that somebody had made for feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:151:Seln went down first, as he went down everything: he walked the old road to the brow and stood there; then he went along the live ruts to the north until he was small, and came back, and went a little way south. When he returned to them his face told nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:183:"Thirty-two," said Lira. She did not crow; she said it as she would have read out the height of a wall. "I said that there'd be one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:239:The quiet one did not let any of them sleep until he had said his order, and he said it at the fire, when it was full dark, with the six of them close about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:249:"We go at first light. We go in file, ten yards apart, down the old road: myself, and then Lira, and Karis, and Cael, with Brom last. The bow is spanned before we cross, with a bolt in the groove, and Brom carries it. Each of us has a day's water and a day's bread on his back. I have known a morning's errand to take a day." His eyes went to the boxes. "Nothing else goes over. The three boxes stay at this stone with both beasts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:275:*Hundred and thirteenth day, night. At the edge of the second one. A stone, and two bars, both numbered thirty-two; one five cold terms old, one a cold term or two, with a mark under its number that Seln won't read. A floor with no stones on it, and a hole, and a stair. Nobody on the ruts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:277:*Tide: once, on the healer, on the ridge road. I asked twice today and she granted the second. The short count and then a minute of the long, at one sitting; I am counting that as one, since it was one asking and one yes. At three there were banks. I knew the Path by ten. In the minute, one channel and one thing in it, going somewhere. It cost her nothing that she would notice. It cost me the use of that hand for an hour, and most of a loaf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:285:They ate cold. Brom put his boot into the stirrup of a healer's bow, and set the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch, and he laid one of the five bolts in the groove. Karis had her lamp and her notebook and a flask, and Lira drew the knot of her sling tight with her teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:23:Lira had the bow off her back. She held it out by the stock, with the one bolt lying in its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:59:There were four of them behind him, and one mule. They stood at the heap of stones as she had left them: a small man with a case on his chest, and a small woman with a hide under her arm, and a very big one bound up like a parcel, and a girl with a bow on her back and one sleeve empty. It was the crew that had come down off a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second day past the Line, and it looked a good deal the worse for wear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:69:Karis had it on the hide at the bend, with her back to the wind. The bright edge lay through her three dots and ran off the skin; and when she lifted her eyes from it, they went along the back of the ridge, which was long, and bare, and going exactly as the brass went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:75:She did not pretend that the line was better than it was. At each halt she had Seln stand a furlong ahead of her, and looked back along the turf to the last place where they had rested; then she moved him a few paces to one hand or the other, with her arm, until the two marks and her own boots lay in a row. It was how a person carries a ruler across country when she has none long enough, and she said that it was good to the width of the strip and no better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:85:Lira walked beside him for a mile in the afternoon, on the frozen turf, with the bow knocking against her back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:93:They slept on the ridge in a hollow, without wood, in every blanket that they had. Brom laid six bowls on the turf. He looked at the row, and took one back; and it passed without remark, since he had done exactly that on a hundred nights.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:101:By noon he could see for thirty miles. There was no road in any of it, and no smoke, and not a fence or a roof; there were hills going away blue behind hills, with the white lying in their folds, and a sky over them so large that he could not look at the whole of it at once. Four people were strung out along the turf ahead of him, small and dark. A man who knew every road out of a town was walking toward a place where there was none. A girl who had been ranked every week of her life was a mile from the nearest thing that could have measured her, with a bow on her back that she could shoot and could not bend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:111:It was true, and he let it stand; and the other thing was in the same coat with it, and had been all morning. They were walking along a ruler. Three floors lay in a row, as stones lie along a path; and when he looked ahead down the ridge, to where the strip went on into the blue, he was more afraid of what was under the brass than he had been of a thing in a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:123:The wind had dropped with the sun. There was no bird over that ground, and no beast on it, and nothing that moved in all the width of it but the last of the light going up the far slope; Cael had stood above two such places, and neither had been a third the size of this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:139:Below the ruts, a furlong down the slope, there was something else going round. It was a line of small dark uprights, set close, running away on both hands with the curve of the ground until the dusk had it. From the crest it looked like a row of stitches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:159:Nobody crossed. Cael came down to her, and the others behind him; and they stood in a row along the outside of that fence, in the last of the light, as people stand at the rail of a bridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:227:They climbed back to the crest and slept there, in the wind, with the valley black below them and nothing lit in it. He wrote by her lamp, when the others were down.

