editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:3:They were out of Lowmarch two hours before the light on the hundred and thirteenth day, with the stars still hard over the river and every blade in the meadow standing white.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:23:It came up to them out of the notch while they were still among the rocks: a slow heavy knocking, with water falling under it, at about the pace of a tired man splitting wood. Brom stopped the mule to listen. Then the track came round a shoulder, and the valley opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:153:"I was to be at this gate on the ninety-sixth. I came in on the hundred and twelfth. That's sixteen." The thumb went down. "There's a day's bench here still, that I'd have done tomorrow, and the road beyond it: two gates, and then the ford with the oxen on the hundred and twenty-first, where they looked for me on the hundred and fourth. That's seventeen." A finger. "Now put your ridge into it. It's a half-day up to wherever this is, and I'd not see the place before dark. A day while you're in it. A half-day down. That's two, if every one of you walks out on his own legs, and you've not once done that since I knew you." Another finger. "So the ford's nineteen. And beyond the ford there's a child that I heard on the eighty-ninth and can't hear now before the hundred and twenty-eighth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:173:She walked to the lean-to's edge and stood looking up the valley, where a hurdle was still in sight, very small, between two boys.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:201:"I'm not coming with you. I've put you on the round. That's a different thing, and I'll not have it mistaken." Her eyes went along the five of them, and they were as level and as tired as he had ever seen them. "You're on the route now. Which means when I say lie still, you lie still, and when I say I can't help you, you believe me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:235:She stood under her three boxes at the fence-post, quite still, with both ears pricked at the square woman in the dead man's coat. The healer did not go to her. She tightened a strap on the grey that did not want tightening.
