editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:175:Cael did the sum, since it was the only thing left to him that was quick. Brom lay two strides from the sill. She had fifteen yards still to cross, hopping. The thing had been at a stone in eleven counts from a third of the dish away, and it had only the width of a room to go. And when she reached him she would stop, since a healer who has come to a fallen man gets down on her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:179:He had ten fragments. He went through nine of them in the time of one breath, as he had on a frozen road, and it was different, because here there was nobody to see, and he need hold none of them back. Wind was in his feet already. It would put him over the wall in a count. Then it would lock him where he landed, dumb, for a count more, the stillest thing on that floor, at a time and a place that he had not chosen. Storm would lay the water flat, and what was that to a thing with no eyes? Ember's fire was for things that could see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:199:It came back along under the ledge faster than he had thought anything of that size could come. He felt the mass of it, and which way it bore, and nothing else; it was as broad as the ledge, and it went on arriving. It had turned in its own length, out in the dish, and left the man who lay still there for the man who had just come down upon its roof.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:211:He was still standing. He had not been sure that he would be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:215:The head lay at the ledge's lip beside his knee, half out of the water, quite still. He could have put his hand on it. It was the brown of the marsh, and smooth, and seamed down the middle where the two halves met; and it had no more face than the toe of a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:219:He had perhaps a count. He did not spend it on the wall. A stone that stopped the thing once was only a stone, and it would go from him to the next weight that lay still, which was lying outside a wall with a healer coming to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:253:She had slid down from her knees to the seat of her breeches on the wall-top, with her left leg folded under her and her right knee drawn up. The sole of the great boot was planted whole on the stone. Behind her right shoulder a length of the wall's old coping still stood, a foot higher than the rest. She set her shoulder against it, and laid the stock across her knee, and drew its butt back into the hollow of that shoulder. The knee and the coping held the bow between them, and her right hand had only a lever to mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:59:She unrolled it on the lid of the box and set a pebble on each corner. There were three rings on it still, and one of them was a lie: she had drawn it on the ninety-second evening round a place where a carter lifted his hand, and since noon she had known to a stride where it ought to have been. She took the crumb of bread that she kept for such work and rubbed the old ring out. She drew the new one a finger nearer to the corner, where a cord and a girl's left foot had put it, and wrote the day beside it, and *moved; paced.*
